{"id": "160662", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=160662", "title": "Alcossebre", "text": "Alcossebre (Spanish: \"Alcoc\u00e9bre\" or \"Alcoc\u00e9ber\") is a seaside village on the Costa del Azahar of Castell\u00f3n province, south of Pe\u00f1iscola. For visitors it is best known for its excellent beaches. It is surrounded by natural parks: Sierra de Irta on the north, Islas Columbretes on the east and Prat de Cabanes on the south. In Serra de Irta there are many small beaches.\nUnderstand.\nThe village of Alcossebre is administratively part of the municipality of the neighbouring inland town of Alcal\u00e0 de Xivert. Traditionally sons would inherit fertile lands around Alcala, while daughters inherited less valuable property on the seafront. Today however the daughters are the winners of this arrangement, as beginning in the 1980s the tranquil fishing village began to develop into a seaside resort.\nThe village has largely kept its Spanish identity, however, by maintaining its charming historic centre with narrow alleys and romantic shops and caf\u00e9s, and by avoiding the worst of the over-development and mass tourism common in other areas of the Spanish coastline. In the summer, its winter population of 5,000 doubles, with mostly Spanish and French visitors as well as a few Germans and Dutch.\nGet in.\nBy bus.\nAutos Mediterraneo[dead link] runs a daily bus connecting Castell\u00f3n directly with Alcossebre; a bus route and schedule can be downloaded here[dead link].\nThe same company also runs multiple daily buses between Castell\u00f3n and Alcal\u00e0 de Xivert, stopping at the train station and at a near the football pitch on Carrer del General Cucala. As an alternate option, and provided you do not have too much luggage, you can then transfer at either of these stops to the local bus service, detailed below in 'get around'. A bus route and schedule can be downloaded here[dead link].\nGet around.\nBy bus.\nAutos Mediterr\u00e1neo (\u260f) operates a route connecting Alcossebre with Alcal\u00e0 de Xivert and points in between. On weekdays, buses run every 15 min from 07:15-22:00, and on weekends and holidays every couple of hours; a schedule is available at the tourist office or can be downloaded here[dead link].\nDo.\nBeaches.\nWith the exception of Tres Platges, the following are designated as blue-flag beaches.\nNatural pebble beaches stretch further south of this point with water clear enough to see fish, and caravans are permitted to camp here. Noteworthy is the kilometre-long nudist beach of (Playa Serradal); dogs are permitted on the beach near the dry mouth of the. The next beach, (\"Playa Cap y Corp\"), terminates near the Disco Tunel nightclub (described below under 'drink'), where there is a footpath to the (\"Playa de Terrenostra\", in the neighbouring village of Terrenostra), with good surfing and few people.\nBuy.\nMost shops, with a few exceptions, close for the midday siesta, which lasts from 14:00/15:00 until 16:00/17:00.\nEat.\nThere are many restaurants and caf\u00e9s, both for sit-down meals and takeaway, which run the gamut of Turkish to Chinese to pizzerias. Especially along the beach promenade of Platja del Carregador there are a number of caf\u00e9s and tapas bars.\nDrink.\nThere are just a few nightclubs in Alcossebre. The most well-known include (\"Les Fonts\") and (\"Capicorb\"), a bit further away from the village centre.\nSleep.\nHoliday rentals.\nThere are many private holiday villas, townhouses, and apartments available for rent, most with private pool and garden. The most upscale and popular neighbourhoods are the areas around Marcolina, Les Fonts (\"Las Fuentes\"), and the Puerto (port).\nBurglary rings are active in more upscale neighbourhoods, including Marcolina and especially Les Fonts. Don't leave valuables on the terrace or balcony table for even a short time. Most houses have alarm systems and security systems, but break-ins are still a frequent occurrence, and on checking in to your villa do not be surprised to see evidence of recent attempted break-ins even at the front door."}
{"id": "160671", "revid": "2428195", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=160671", "title": "Larapinta Trail", "text": "The Larapinta Trail is a long-distance walking trail in the southern Red Centre region of the Northern Territory of Australia. It runs for over 230 km through the rugged ranges and desert landscapes west of Alice Springs.\nUnderstand.\nThe Larapinta Trail runs from the Alice Springs Telegraph Station to the summit of Mount Sonder, following much of the spine of the MacDonnell Ranges through Tjoritja / West MacDonnell National Park.\nThe route is divided into 12 official sections. Some can be completed in a day, while the longer and more difficult sections are normally split over two days.\nPrepare.\nThe trail crosses remote, dry and often rugged country. Walkers should be comfortable carrying several days of supplies, camping equipment and sufficient drinking water.\nEat.\nThere are very limited opportunities to buy food along the trail, so walkers attempting longer sections should carry most of what they need.\nFood drops can be arranged at some road-accessible trailheads, which can reduce the amount that needs to be carried at one time.\nDrink.\nReliable natural surface water should not be assumed. Water tanks are provided at a number of trailheads and campsites, but availability can vary and walkers should check current conditions before departure.\nCarry enough water between reliable sources, especially on exposed and difficult sections.\nSleep.\nOvernight walkers must use designated campsites. There are more than 20 designated campsites along the route, including Wallaby Gap, Simpsons Gap, Mulga Camp, Jay Creek, Brinkley Bluff, Hugh Gorge, Ellery Creek, Serpentine Gorge, Waterfall Gorge, Ormiston Gorge, Finke River and Redbank Gorge.\nCampsites must be booked in advance. Standley Chasm has a privately operated campground.\nWalkers should carry suitable camping equipment for cold desert nights and exposed campsites.\nClimate.\nThe usual walking season is during the cooler months, roughly May to September. June to August generally offers the coolest daytime conditions.\nDaytime temperatures can still be warm, while winter nights can become very cold, particularly at higher or exposed campsites.\nSummer conditions can be dangerously hot and are unsuitable for most long-distance walking on the trail.\nGet in.\nAlice Springs is the usual eastern starting point. The trail begins at the Alice Springs Telegraph Station, a short distance north of the town centre.\nWalk.\nStart at.\nThe first section is graded moderate to difficult, with some steep climbs. The route gives wide views over Alice Springs and the surrounding plains.\n is an intermediate campsite with tank water.\nFinish at.\nThe trail crosses rocky terrain and low ranges west of Simpsons Gap.\nPass, an intermediate campsite with tank water.\nContinue through the area around.\nFinish at.\nDespite being relatively short, this section crosses some steep and rugged country.\nTwo route variants may be available, including a higher and more difficult route and a lower route.\nFinish at. The campground here is privately operated.\nThis is one of the more difficult parts of the trail.\nThe track climbs onto the quartzite ridges of the Chewings Range and reaches, one of the major viewpoints on the Larapinta Trail.\nFrom Brinkley Bluff the route descends into Stuarts Pass and follows the Hugh River valley westward.\nDesignated campsites include Brinkley Bluff, Stuarts Pass and the Section 4\u20135 Junction.\nThe official section finishes near Birthday Waterhole.\nThis is a difficult section.\nThe trail passes Spencer Gorge, follows Razorback Ridge and descends through Linear Valley toward the Hugh River.\nDepending on recent conditions, walkers may have to wade through pools in Hugh Gorge.\nDesignated camping is available at Fringe Lily Creek and Hugh Gorge.\nFinish at.\nThis is one of the longest sections of the trail.\nThe route leaves the Chewings Range, crosses the broad Alice Valley and approaches the Heavitree Range.\nMany walkers divide this section into two days using an intermediate campsite.\nFinish at.\nThe relatively gently undulating route offers extensive views across the Alice Valley before passing through the Heavitree Range.\nFinish at.\nThe route follows rocky ridges and offers views toward the higher peaks of the western MacDonnell Ranges.\nFinish at.\nThis is one of the most difficult sections of the Larapinta Trail.\nThe trail traverses rugged ridge country in the western ranges. There is no reliable surface water along the section, so walkers must carry enough water for the journey.\nMost walkers divide the section over two days, camping at.\nThe route then continues toward, a major trailhead with camping facilities.\nThis is the shortest official section of the Larapinta Trail and is graded moderate.\nThe trail passes through limestone hills near the headwaters of the Finke River.\nFinish at.\nThe route crosses spinifex-covered hills with increasingly close views of Mount Sonder. It then descends toward Rocky Bar Gap before following the southern side of Mount Sonder.\n is a useful intermediate camping point.\nFinish at.\nThe final section is a return climb from Redbank Gorge to the summit of Mount Sonder.\nThe climb is steep and sustained but gives extensive views across the surrounding ranges, plains and valleys.\nReach before returning to Redbank Gorge.\nSome walkers begin the climb before dawn in order to reach the higher slopes around sunrise. Anyone doing so should carry a head torch and take particular care with navigation.\nStay safe.\nThe Larapinta Trail passes through isolated desert country, and weather can change rapidly.\nIt is generally recommended to walk during the cooler months, particularly from June to August. Summer temperatures can be extreme and attempting long sections in very hot weather can be dangerous.\nDehydration and heat illness are major risks. Carry sufficient water and do not rely on natural waterholes. Check the condition of water tanks before setting out.\nHypothermia is also possible. Winter nights can become very cold, particularly at exposed campsites such as Brinkley Bluff.\nThe trail is generally well marked, but walkers should still carry a detailed map or other suitable navigation aid. A wrong turn in remote terrain can quickly become serious.\nSnakes occur in the region. Avoid placing hands or feet into places you cannot see, and take care around rocks, long grass and warm exposed ground.\nMobile phone coverage is unreliable or absent along much of the route. For a full traverse, carrying a satellite communicator or personal locator beacon is sensible.\nBefore departure, check current trail conditions, closures, water availability and fire restrictions with Northern Territory Parks.\nGo next.\nReturn by road from Redbank Gorge toward Alice Springs, or continue exploring Tjoritja / West MacDonnell National Park."}
{"id": "160692", "revid": "2253620", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=160692", "title": "Working in the United States", "text": "The United States, as the largest economy in the world, entices foreigners with employment opportunities across the full range of skill levels and economic sectors. Like most countries, though, the U.S. has adopted immigration and visa laws designed to give preference to its own residents. Make sure you understand what legal barriers you face to getting a job in the U.S.\nVisa requirements.\nCitizens of U.S. overseas territories are considered to be U.S. nationals and may work unrestricted in the United States. Citizens of Palau, Marshall Islands and the Federated States of Micronesia are allowed to live and work indefinitely in the United States with only a valid passport. For citizens of all other countries, including Canadians and Bermudians, unless you are a permanent resident of the United States (i.e. a green card holder), you will need a work visa to be allowed to work in the United States. Paid work is not allowed on a B-1/B-2 visitor visa or the visa-waiver program. Volunteer work is allowed provided the position taken up is a legitimate volunteer position (i.e. it would ordinarily be done unpaid by a local). Working unlawfully in the United States runs the very real risk of arrest, deportation, and ineligibility to re-enter the country. Illegal immigrants also run the risk of dangerous work conditions.\nTaxes and payments.\nMost employers deposit their employees' salaries directly into their bank accounts, so it is advisable to open a bank account as soon as you arrive in the U.S.\nOnce you have received work authorization, you will have to apply for a Social Security Number (SSN) for your employer to report your wages to the government for tax purposes. You can start working before you receive your number, but you must provide your employer with a receipt from the Social Security Administration indicating that you have applied for one, and you will need to provide the number to your employer once you have received it.\nProvide your SSN to your bank once you obtain it, as your bank will need to use it to report any interest you accrue to the government for tax purposes. Similarly, your credit history is tied to your SSN in the U.S., so you will need it to apply for credit cards, loans, and the like. Make sure to keep this number private, as it can easily be used for \"identity theft\", if it falls into the wrong hands.\nThe American tax year runs from January 1 to December 31, and tax returns for the previous year are due on April 15 (or the next working day, if it falls on a weekend or public holiday) every year. Income tax in the United States is notoriously complicated, with taxpayers generally having to file separate federal, state and sometimes city income tax returns. Most Americans simplify things for themselves by paying a tax preparer or using tax preparation software such as H&amp;R Block, TaxAct, or TurboTax. (Non-residents who need to use form 1040NR can use Sprintax as TurboTax doesn't support it.) If you don't want to pay for these services, you can also use Free File for your federal tax returns, which requires doing more of the math yourself but is, you guessed it, free. Some community organizations also provide tax preparation assistance for non-citizens living in the country. Top earners can pay surprisingly low taxes by hiring a tax accountant to take advantage of complicated deductions and a lower tax rate for capital gains; American multibillionaire Warren Buffett famously announced that he paid a lower tax rate than his secretary.\nThis type of tax avoidance is very common and socially accepted. Illegal tax \"evasion\" is a different story: the U.S. has one of the highest voluntary tax compliance rates in the world. Tax evasion carries social disapproval, as well as fines and jail time if you're caught. Do not evade taxes.\nWork rights on student visas.\nUnless you have applied for and received special permission in advance, international students are \"not\" allowed to work off-campus in the United States. For on-campus jobs, international students may work for up to 20 hours a week during the school term, and full time during the school holidays.\nTypes of work visas.\nIt is better to arrange your work and work visa before you enter the United States. Young people who are full-time students of certain nationalities can apply for a J-1 \"Exchange Visitor\" visa[dead link] which permits paid work as a nanny or summer work for up to 4 months in virtually any type of job. The United States Department of State has full information on applying for this type of visa including the precise categories that qualify.\nThe H-1B visa allows a limited number of skilled and certain unskilled employees to temporarily work in the United States. It usually requires at least a bachelor's degree and is based on a petition filed by an American employer. The job you wish to apply for should be related to your degree. The most common careers of hard-to-get H-1B visa holders are nurses, mathematics teachers, and computer science professionals. There is also the L-1 visa which allow individuals who are employees of multinational companies to be transferred to the U.S. office, provided you are employed in an executive or managerial position, or as a worker with \"specialized knowledge\". The O-1 and P-1 visas exist for performing artists and sportspeople to perform their respective trades in the U.S., though in practice you will need to be of celebrity status for it to be approved. Individuals who hold a PhD in scientific or engineering fields may work for an educational institution in a research position on a J-1 visa for up to 5 years. Dependents of L-1 and J-1 visa holders may apply for permission to work in the U.S., while dependents of H-1B, O-1 and P-1 visa holders are not allowed to work in the U.S. under any circumstances. Before an employer can arrange any work visa, he must ensure that nobody within his locale is willing or qualified to do the job before considering you.\nCanadian citizens who have secured job offers in certain skilled professions (those outlined in the USMCA trade agreement as professionals under its labor mobility provisions) are eligible to apply for TN status on arrival without much of the difficulty that comes with applying for an H-1B visa. You will only need documentation of your job offer from your employer, proof of your own qualifications, and your Canadian passport or other proof of Canadian citizenship. Once admitted, this status is valid for three years and can be renewed provided you continue to work for the same employer. Mexican citizens who have secured qualifying employment, along with Canadians with non-Canadian spouses or children who they wish to bring into the US on dependent status, must instead apply in advance for a TN visa at a US consulate or embassy, but as with Canadians applying for TN status on arrival, applicants for TN visas are not subject to hard limits or any requirement that the employer must have been unable to find a local hire. \nIf you are seeking to adjust visa status or to enter the U.S. on a working visa you should first check the official government websites of the U.S. Department of State, which issues visas abroad, and the U.S. Citizenship and Immigration Services which administers immigration programs within the United States. Unfortunately, con artists both in the U.S. and overseas often prey on people's desire to travel or work in the U.S. Keep in mind that visa applications do not usually require an attorney or other intermediary, so you should be wary of and verify any \"advice\" offered by third parties, especially non-lawyers. If in doubt about properly applying for such visas then it is recommended to use a licensed immigration attorney.\nNo-one entering under the Visa Waiver Program is permitted to adjust their status for any reason. For detailed immigration rules related to working in the U.S., visit the web-site of the Bureau of Consular Affairs[dead link].\nPermanent residency.\nAs the H-1B, L-1, O-1 and P-1 visas are considered to be dual-intent visas, foreigners working on those visas may apply for permanent residency, which if granted, allows them to stay in the U.S. indefinitely and change jobs freely. On the other hand, the J-1 visa and TN visa are strict non-immigrant visas, meaning that applying for a green card could potentially be viewed as immigrant intent, and lead to you being deported from the U.S. for breaching your visa conditions. Applying for permanent residency will automatically make you ineligible to receive most non-immigrant visas in the future, the exception being visas that specifically allow for dual-intent.\nThe easiest way to obtain permanent residency is to be sponsored by your employer. You may also file an immigrant petition for yourself, though you will generally have to be exceptional, and demonstrate that granting you permanent residency is beneficial to the U.S. in order for it to be granted. Permanent residency can also be granted by being married to a U.S. citizen or permanent resident for at least 2 years. If you have a lot of money, permanent residency can also be obtained by investing at least $1,000,000 in a local business, provided your investment leads to the creation of at least 10 new jobs for U.S. citizens. Once you have been living in the U.S. as a permanent resident for 5 years (3 years if you are married to a U.S. citizen) or more, you are eligible to apply for U.S. citizenship.\nWorker protections and time off.\nCompared to many other middle- and high-income countries, U.S. labor laws tend to favor the employer; protections for workers are relatively weak. This means you will need to read your employment agreement carefully, as legal requirements that you might take for granted back home (for working hours, time off, termination of employment, and so on) may be limited or nonexistent in the United States.\nThere is no legal requirement for American employers to provide paid leave to their employees, be it medical, maternity or annual leave. However, these are typically provided by most large companies and universities in their employment contracts. Ensure that any leave your employer agrees to give you is explicitly stated in your employment agreement, as you will have no legal recourse otherwise. While government bodies and banks are required to observe public holidays, private businesses may freely choose whether or not to do so.\nMany jobs in the US don't use formal contracts, but instead \"offer letters\", which may be legally binding but are typically less comprehensive than employment contracts in many other countries. Conditions such as non-disclosure clauses and paid leave may be covered in separate documents. There may not be a formal notice period for resignation, but unless otherwise specified, two weeks' notice is considered standard.\nHealthcare.\nThe United States is the only Western country that does not have a taxpayer-funded universal free healthcare system that covers all citizens. Most middle-to-upper-class Americans have private health insurance, the cost of which is usually borne by their employer; whether poorer Americans are adequately covered varies wildly between states. Healthcare costs in the United States are the highest in the world, so ensure that your employment contract comes with health insurance, as the cost of medical treatment while uninsured is prohibitively expensive for most people (with medical bills being the leading cause of bankruptcy among Americans). Drug prices are the highest in the world; the price of insulin, for instance, is almost 10 times that in Canada.\nThat said, the United States is a leading nation in medical research, you have at your disposal the most cutting edge treatments that are often not available anywhere else, assuming your insurance covers them.\nWorkplace culture.\nWhen it comes to clothing, business casual (slacks, understated collared shirts without a tie, and non-athletic shoes) is now the default at many companies. More traditional industries (e.g. law and insurance) still require suits and ties, and three-piece suits may be required in some formal settings (e.g. if you need to go to court). Other industries (e.g. computer software) are even more casual, allowing a T-shirt and jeans and even shorts for everyday wear; as a business visitor, a safe choice would be business casual, or jeans and a collared shirt. If you're starting a new job in an office environment, it's fine to ask how people at the company usually dress."}
