{"id": "164027", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=164027", "title": "Barry's Bay", "text": "Barry's Bay is a town of about 1,300 people in the Township of Madawaska Valley in Eastern Ontario, near the eastern entrance to Algonquin Provincial Park.\nUnderstand.\nHistory.\nThis area of Renfrew County was first settled by non-Indigenous people in the early 20th century along with other communities on the Opeongo Line. The town served as a standby base for the Canadian military during the war years. Local workers and lumbermen were reformed into soldiers to help contribute to the war efforts of Canada in World War II.\nIt was first named 'Kuaenash Ne-ishing' (Algonquin for 'beautiful bay'), and later named after James Barry, a foreman for McLaughlin's Lumber in Arnprior who constructed a cabin as his headquarters in the area where St. Hedwig's Church stands.\nGet in.\nBarry's Bay is at the northern end of Ontario Highway 62, at its junction with Ontario Highway 60. It is 185 km west of Ottawa - follow Highways 417 and 17 to Highway 60 near Renfrew.\nGet around.\nThe nearest taxis are in Pembroke (Ontario).\nSleep.\nAs the second-closest town east of Algonquin Provincial Park (Whitney is right at the park's entrance), Barry's Bay gets quite a few overnight visitors who are tired of camping in the park."}
{"id": "164033", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=164033", "title": "Karaman", "text": "Karaman is an industrial city in Central Anatolia, Turkey, with a population in 2012 of 141,630. It has an interesting medieval centre, with a castle and several mosques and museums, and it's also a stopover on the overland routes to Adana and Cyprus. \nUnderstand.\nKaraman stands at an altitude of 1000 m on the fertile plateau of central Anatolia. Its livelihood has always depended on agriculture and today it's a major centre for durum wheat (which is ground up to make bulgur), for pulses and fruit, and for biscuits and other confectionery. In ancient times it was known as Laranda (Greek: \"\u039b\u03ac\u03c1\u03b1\u03bd\u03b4\u03b1\") and many invaders thought it was worth seizing, including the Roman / Byzantine Empire. But the fellow who most put his stamp on the city was Karamano\u011flu Mehmet Bey, Mehmet the 1st, who in 1256 founded a Karamid dynasty and something of a buffer state in this turbulent region. He briefly held Konya and famously proclaimed Turkish as the official language, not Persian or Arabic. He was soon hunted down by his enemies but the Karamid state continued until 1468, when it was conquered by the Ottomans. The regional capital moved to Konya, and Karaman became a dusty neglected place, which meant that its medieval centre was left intact.\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nThe nearest airports are Konya and \u00c7ukurova (near Tarsus), all with at least daily flights from Istanbul.\nBy train.\nHigh-speed YHT trains run twice a day from Ankara (3 hr) and Istanbul (6 hr), both via Konya (50 min) and \u00c7umra. \n\"Toros Express\" runs each morning from Adana, taking 6 hr 30 min to Karaman and continuing to Konya. It makes multiple stops including \u00c7umra and returns east in the afternoon. \nFor timetables and tickets see TCDD.\n is north edge of old town.\nBy bus.\nThe main highway D715 between Konya (100 km north) and Mersin passes through Karaman. Here Highway D350 branches east via Eregdi to Kayseri and points east. The is at this junction, about 1 km west of town centre.\nBuses operated by \u00d6zkaymak run frequently direct to Istanbul (12 hours), Ankara (6 hours), Antalya (8 hours), Konya (2 hr 30 min) and Adana (4 hours). \nTheir buses also run frequently south to Silifke (90 min). Change there for a dolmus to Ta\u015fucu, the nearby ferry port. For foot passengers only, a hydrofoil runs May-Sept 3 times a week to Girne (Kyrenia) in Northern Cyprus. An overnight car ferry runs year-round, leaving both ports 3 times a week at midnight to arrive at 06:00. See Northern Cyprus \u201cget in\u201d for current timetable and fares.\nGet around.\nWalking will get you to all points of interest within the city. Dolmuses ply all the main streets.\nYou need your own transport for the caves and canyons beyond the city, or join an organised tour, or negotiate a taxi to take you out there for a few hours.\nEat.\nPlenty of cheap eating places along Ismet Pa\u015fa Cad running east from the museum, and Istasyon Cad running north.\nConnect.\nAs of Feb 2021, Karaman has 4G from all Turkish carriers. 5G has not reached this area.\nGo next.\nFor Istanbul, Ankara, Konya, Eski\u015fehir, Adana and Cyprus follow directions in \u201cGet in\u201d.\nFor Goreme in Cappadocia, with its fairy-chimney landscape, travel to Konya whence a bus runs 4 times a day, 3 hours."}
{"id": "164035", "revid": "144844", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=164035", "title": "Akka", "text": "Akka is a town in Anti Atlas, Morocco.\nUnderstand.\nAkka is a small desert town in the Tata province surrounded by its oases. It has been said to be one of the northernmost depots of the ancient caravan routes. With its red clay houses, Akka serves itself as a trading centre.\nSee.\nThe local sights around Akka include an ancient Kasbah and Agadir Azro, a fortress which served itself as a food storage facility. Other attractions near Akka is a spring and shallow pools that bring life-giving water to the village."}
{"id": "164044", "revid": "2432955", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=164044", "title": "Akwidaa", "text": "Akwidaa is in the Ghanaian Coastal Plain.\nUnderstand.\nAkwidaa is one of the southernmost places in Ghana. The charming fishing village is surrounded by its unique, long pristine sand beaches.\nThe town was once called Ezile, named after for the river. During the Dutch occupation, the town was renamed after an old man who used to ferry people across the river.\nHistory.\nThe coast around Akwidaa has long been inhabited by coastal communities whose livelihoods were connected to fishing, farming and trade.\nEuropean trading activity along the western Ghanaian coast increased from the 15th century onwards. The wider coastline became an important area for European merchants because of its access to gold and other commodities from the interior.\nAlthough Akwidaa is a relatively small settlement, its history is connected with the broader history of the Ahanta coast, which includes several historic European forts and trading settlements. The village has retained much of its traditional coastal character, with fishing continuing to be an important part of the local economy.\nClimate.\nAkwidaa has a warm and humid tropical coastal climate. Temperatures remain relatively high throughout the year, while the proximity of the Atlantic Ocean contributes to high humidity.\nRainfall is seasonal, with wetter periods during parts of the year. The surrounding vegetation remains relatively lush because western Ghana receives more rainfall than many areas farther north. The coast can experience strong waves and currents, particularly during rough weather, so visitors should exercise caution when entering the sea.\nGet around.\nAkwidaa is small enough to explore largely on foot. Walking is also a good way to experience the village, coastline and surrounding scenery. Local taxis and other forms of informal transport can be used for longer journeys to neighbouring communities.\nSee.\nExplore cocoa plantations, hike in the forests, see humpback whales, take a guided night-time tour of the turtle nesting spots on the beach, visit the Cape Three Points, the southernmost point in Ghana."}
{"id": "164068", "revid": "4696365", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=164068", "title": "Pomerode", "text": "Pomerode is a city in the Itaja\u00ed Valley, in Santa Catarina State, Southern Brazil. Settled in the 19th century by German immigrants from the historical region of Pomerania (which also includes part of current Poland), it is considered to be the most authentic German town in Brazil.\nUnderstand.\nIn many places in the city, there are houses built according to the typical half-timbered Germanic architecture, German food restaurants and parties that celebrate the Germanic traditions, carefully preserved by its inhabitants.\nGet in.\nThere are regular busses from Blumenau on weekdays, which take about 1 hour. A private transfer from Blumenau is about 60-70 R$ (more during Oktoberfest in Blumenau).\nDo.\nEvery year, in January, the Pomeranian Festival takes place in the city, a festival known for its focus on German culture, much like the Oktoberfest. There are many aspects of the local culture that take place at these celebrations, such as the traditional Pomeranian wedding ritual, typical food and drink, and German music.\nThere is a craft fair, selling local and regional products: clothing, the traditional cucas, chocolates, and sausages, with emphasis on \"Fensterbilder\" (window pictures), produced in the city. You can find articles of typical German clothing, mugs, straps, hats, and buttons for decoration.\nCompetitions are held, such as the draft beer, wood sawing competition, target shooting, and the Miss da Festa Pomeranian contest. Each edition brings together around 25,000 people and lasts for almost 2 weeks.\nThere are also other parties, such as Osterfest (Easter), Christmas, and the Flea Market (used objects fair)."}
{"id": "164070", "revid": "132185", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=164070", "title": "Brotas", "text": "Brotas is a city and a municipality in the S\u00e3o Paulo state, Brazil. It is a popular destination in the state for ecotourism, particularly rafting at various difficulty levels.\nUnderstand.\nBrotas is a small town (city) Cathedral in the state of S\u00e3o Paulo not far from the cities of Araraquara and S\u00e3o Carlos. It has a population of about 23,000 people.\nGet in.\nTo arrive in Brotas you can go by car or by bus, because there is a bus station, but it's more advisable to go by car.\nGet around.\nBecause Brotas is a small town, it is easy to walk by foot, or even hire a bicycle.\nDo.\nThere are many activities to do like whitewater rafting, canoeing, zip-lining and a swim in Areia que Canta pools. Or do canopy.\nBuy.\nYou can buy souvenirs and t-shirts of rafting and of the bars that sponsor the outdoor activities.\nEat.\nYou can eat at the restaurants or at Brotas Bar (a famous bar).\nDrink.\nThere are many bars through the main two streets."}
{"id": "164071", "revid": "43226", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=164071", "title": "Jacupiranga Mosaic", "text": "The Jacupiranga Mosaic is a collection of conservation units in the state of S\u00e3o Paulo, Brazil. It is part of the \"Atlantic South-East Reserves\", a UNESCO World Natural Heritage Site.\nUnderstand.\nThe Jacupiranga Mosaic is in the Ribeira Valley and the south coast of the state of S\u00e3o Paulo, with 14 conservation units of various categories. It was created to reconcile the goals of conserving the Atlantic Forest and improving the living conditions of the traditional populations of the area. It originated with the Jacupiranga State Park, created on 8 August 1969, and includes the Rio Turvo and Caverna do Diabo state parks. It also includes four environmental protection areas, five sustainable development reserves and two extractive reserves.\nIt cover a large, mountainous region that included untouched Atlantic Forest and land occupied by traditional communities. \nFlora and fauna.\nThe area contains large tracts of Atlantic Forest. Vegetation includes dense rainforest, dense rainforest with limestone outcrops, restinga forest and sandy restinga. \nGet in.\nBy BR-116 (Rod. R\u00e9gis Bitencourt) - Headquarters at Km 543.\nIt is 230 km away from the capital, S\u00e3o Paulo, and 35 km from Registro.\nSee.\nThe Caverna do Diabo State Park has an extensive cave system, the Devil's Cave being the main one. This cave is 6,000 m long, but only 600 m are open to visitors. It has a lighting system. The park is home to endangered species such as the black-faced lion tamarin and the jaguar.\nThe Rio Turvo State Park is important for the maintenance of marine ecosystems and habitat of the critically endangered black-faced lion tamarin and presents diverse flora, preserving the Turvo river basin, a tributary of the Ribeira do Iguape river."}
{"id": "164089", "revid": "2248136", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=164089", "title": "Bel\u00e9n (Paraguay)", "text": "Bel\u00e9n is a town in Northern Paraguay, 25 km east of Concepci\u00f3n.\nUnderstand.\nThe city of Bel\u00e9n was founded by Jesuit missionaries next to the Ypan\u00e9 River but has strong indigenous Mbya roots in the region."}
