{"id": "164363", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=164363", "title": "Simpsonville", "text": "Simpsonville is a suburban city in the Upcountry of South Carolina southeast of the major city of Greenville. Simpsonville has experienced steady growth since 2008, and as a result, many restaurants and stores have been introduced to the area. Simpsonville has around 20,000 residents.\nUnderstand.\nHistory.\nSimpsonville developed in the 1820s as a stagecoach stop, but eventually became the vast suburban town it is today. The once-burgeoning town struggled during the Civil War and Reconstruction, but in 1885 lots were laid out to surround a new rail line. The Woodside Cotton Mill, established in the early 1900s, was instrumental in the town's growth, as was the installation of a large water main from Greenville. Urban sprawl from the city of Greenville has led to tremendous growth in and around Simpsonville.\nGet in.\nSimpsonville is accessible from Interstate 385 by taking exits 23-31 from Columbia and Greenville.\nGreenlink Route 601 offers bus service Monday\u2013Saturday from Greenville.\nGet around.\nSimpsonville is a large, spread-out town which is best accessible by car. However, areas such as downtown are both bike and pedestrian friendly."}
{"id": "164364", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=164364", "title": "Five Forks", "text": "Five Forks is a community and major suburb of the city of Greenville in the Upcountry of South Carolina. Five Forks is southeast of Greenville with a population of around 15,000.\nUnderstand.\nFive Forks was originally named for the major five-fork intersection in the center of town. The roads have been altered to create a series of three successive intersections. Five Forks is primarily a residential suburb of Greenville. All addresses are based on the closest post office in Simpsonville, but Five Forks is well outside the city limits of Simpsonville. The two elementary schools in Five Forks, Bells Crossing and Monarch, consistently rank as the best elementary schools in the state.\nGet in.\nFive Forks is named after the five primary roads that enter the town. From the West, Woodruff Road connects to Greenville. To the North, Batesville Road, which becomes Pelham Road, connects to the airport and the busy intersection of Interstate 85 and Pelham. To the east, Bennet's Bridge Road passes through Sugar Tit and Reidville on the way to Spartanburg. To the south, Adams Mill Road connects to Simpsonville. Scuffletown Road also heads southward to the neighborhoods west of Simpsonville and Fountain Inn.\nGet around.\nThe Greenville bus service does not extend to Five Forks.\nSleep.\nThere are no hotels or motels in the town of Five Forks. The Airport is just north of Five Forks, with many hotels nearby."}
{"id": "164366", "revid": "2302739", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=164366", "title": "The Most Beautiful Villages of France", "text": "The Most Beautiful Villages of France (\"Les Plus Beaux Villages de France\") is a French association created to promote the tourist value of small rural villages rich of a notable heritage. In September 2021, it had 164 members (whole communes or villages dependent on one).\nThe membership obeys a certain number of selective criteria, defined in the statutes of the association, and proposes a program of enhancement and tourist communication.\nChampagne-Ardenne.\nNone\nNord-Pas-de-Calais.\nNone\nPays de la Loire.\nLoire-Atlantique.\nNone\nSarthe.\nNone"}
